{"qid":"Q6201794","names":["Former Jin"],"title":"Former Jin","endonym":null,"capsule":"The state of Jin (晉) was one of the most powerful of the Spring and Autumn and early Warring States polities, occupying the central Yellow River basin in modern Shanxi and adjacent territories. Founded as a Zhou fief, Jin reached its apex of influence under Duke Wen (r. 636–628 BCE), who became hegemon (bà) of the Zhou states after his victory at Chengpu in 632 BCE; for the next century Jin alternated with Chu for dominance of the interstate system. The state was progressively hollowed out by its powerful ministerial families—the Fan, Zhonghang, Zhi, Wei, Han, and Zhao clans—whose internecine wars culminated in the Partition of Jin between the Wei, Han, and Zhao families, a process completed by Zhou royal recognition in 403 BCE and fully consummated by 376 BCE.","attestation":"Boundaries from Seshat Cliopatria (Bennett et al., Scientific Data 2025; CC BY 4.0), digitized from standard historical atlases at ~0.07° resolution. Core territorial extent is reasonably attested through Zuozhuan and Shiji records, though exact border demarcations at any given year are scholarly reconstructions.","attestationLinks":[{"label":"Cliopatria dataset (Seshat, CC BY 4.0)","url":"https://github.com/Seshat-Global-History-Databank/cliopatria"},{"label":"Bennett et al. 2025, Scientific Data","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-025-04516-9"}],"citations":[{"label":"Britannica: Jin — Chinese state","url":"https://www.britannica.com/place/Jin-Chinese-state"},{"label":"Wikipedia: Jin (Chinese state)","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jin_(Chinese_state)"}],"britannica":null,"cowCode":null,"wikipedia":"Jin (Later Tang precursor)","rulers":[{"qid":null,"name":"Tang Shu Yu","nativeName":"唐叔虞","roman":"Táng Shū Yú","start":-1033,"end":-1007,"image":null,"imageUrl":null,"imageLicense":null},{"qid":null,"name":"Xie (Marquess of Jin)","nativeName":"晋侯燮","roman":"Jìn Hóu Xiè","start":-1007,"end":-97